  "slug": "adna-alignment-sensitivity-dbd7",
  "summary": "Six works target alignment, graph mapping, assembly and k-mer counting bottlenecks with algorithmic redesign or RISC-V and ASIC co-design.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is reducing a specific computational cost in sequence processing through algorithmic redesign or algorithm-hardware co-design. For alignment, minibwa pairs BWA-MEM seeding with minimap2 chaining and is reported ~4x faster than BWA-MEM at comparable accuracy, while lWFA and lBiWFA extend wavefront alignment to exact local alignment in O(sN) time with reduced memory. VEGA (RISC-V vector extension) and Motivo (motif-indexed ASIC for sequence-to-graph mapping) move alignment into hardware. Gallifrey applies locally consistent de Bruijn graphs to HiFi assembly, reported >6x faster than rust-mdbg with 2x less memory. Tuna uses a cost model and minimizer partitioning to revisit sort versus hash k-mer counting, reported faster than KMC3.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
